{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And if a man sells his daughter as a maidservant.</b> The verse speaks of a minor daughter; one might say even if she has brought signs [of puberty] — you would argue a fortiori: if one already sold goes free by signs — as it is written ״וְיָצְאָה חִנָּם אֵין כָּסֶף,״ which we interpret as referring to the signs of youth — then one who is not sold, is it not logical that she not be sold (Arakhin 29)." "<b>She shall not go out as the slaves go out.</b> Like the going out of Canaanite slaves, who go out by tooth and eye — but this one does not go out by tooth and eye, but rather serves for six years, or until the Jubilee, or until she brings signs, and whichever comes first takes precedence for her freedom, and he gives her the payment for her eye or the payment for her tooth. Or is it perhaps only that she shall not go out as the slaves go out — in six years and the Jubilee? Scripture says ״כִּי יִמָּכֵר לְךָ אָחִיךָ הָעִבְרִי אוֹ הָעִבְרִיָּה״ — it juxtaposes the Hebrew woman to the Hebrew man for all her goings out: just as the Hebrew man goes out in six years and the Jubilee, so too the Hebrew woman goes out in six years and the Jubilee. And what is ״she shall not go out as the slaves go out״? She shall not go out missing limbs, as Canaanite slaves do. One might say the Hebrew man goes out missing limbs? Scripture says ״הָעִבְרִי אוֹ הָעִבְרִיָּה״ — it juxtaposes the Hebrew man to the Hebrew woman: just as the Hebrew woman does not go out missing limbs, so too he does not go out missing limbs (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:31.519826987Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}